{"sourceSha256": "3b7166fec777202cb83093f98c74687af08cf11fcacd953603e01ea6176ffe13", "text": "| Test3 | Speaking (approximately 20 minutes)\n\nOET SAMPLE TEST\nROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 1\n\nSETTING\n\nMEDICINE\n\nDoctor's Clinic\n\nPATIENT You are 32 years old and were diagnosed with adult-onset asthma one year ago. Dust\nmites are a major trigger for your asthma. Today you are seeing your doctor for your\nannual asthma review.\n\nTASK e When asked, say you’ve been waking up coughing and wheezing. You've just stoppey\nplaying sport because it makes you wheeze, and you sometimes feel short of breath\nduring the day.\n\n¢ When asked, say you think you’ve reduced your exposure to dust mites in your home\nbecause you now use allergy covers on your mattress, you removed the carpets, ang\nyou regularly wipe down surfaces.\n\ne When asked, say you finished your preventer medication a few months ago and |\nhaven't bought a replacement. You use your reliever when you have symptoms, which\nhelps a little bit.\n\ne Say the preventer inhaler didn’t seem to help you very much, so you don't think you\nneed to start using it again.\n\n¢ Say you didn’t realise there were risks to using the reliever inhaler frequently. You'll\nfollow the advice and buy a new preventer inhaler.\n\ne Say you'll come back in four weeks.\n\n© Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessment (2022)\n\nOET SAMPLE TEST\nCANDIDATE CARD NO. 1 MEDICINE\n\nSETTING Doctor's Clinic\n\nDOCTOR This 32-year-old patient was diagnosed with adult-onset asthma one year ago. Dust mites\nwere identified as a major trigger. You are seeing the patient for his/her annual asthma review.\n\nTASK ¢ Find out about patient’s asthma symptoms (e.g., any change, effect on daily activities!\nsleep, etc.).\n\ne Review patient’s asthma triggers (e.g., exposure to dust mites, any control measures\ntried, etc.).\n\n¢ Explore patient's asthma self-management strategies (e.g., daily use of inhaled\ncorticosteroid preventer, frequency of salbutamol reliever use, etc.).\n\n¢ Explain risks of overusing salbutamol reliever (e.g., increased airway hyper-responsiveness,\nuncontrolled asthma, asthma-related hospital admission more likely, etc.). Advise patient to\npurchase replacement preventer.\n\ne Emphasise importance of using preventer daily (e.g., reduced swelling/inflammation in\nairways, lowered sensitivity to triggers, decreased mucus production, etc.).\n\ne Recommend follow-up appointment in four weeks (e.g., monitor treatment effect,\nreview medication, etc.).", "pages": [{"number": 1, "text": "| Test3 | Speaking (approximately 20 minutes)\n\nOET SAMPLE TEST\nROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 1\n\nSETTING\n\nMEDICINE\n\nDoctor's Clinic\n\nPATIENT You are 32 years old and were diagnosed with adult-onset asthma one year ago. Dust\nmites are a major trigger for your asthma. Today you are seeing your doctor for your\nannual asthma review.\n\nTASK e When asked, say you’ve been waking up coughing and wheezing. You've just stoppey\nplaying sport because it makes you wheeze, and you sometimes feel short of breath\nduring the day.\n\n¢ When asked, say you think you’ve reduced your exposure to dust mites in your home\nbecause you now use allergy covers on your mattress, you removed the carpets, ang\nyou regularly wipe down surfaces.\n\ne When asked, say you finished your preventer medication a few months ago and |\nhaven't bought a replacement. You use your reliever when you have symptoms, which\nhelps a little bit.\n\ne Say the preventer inhaler didn’t seem to help you very much, so you don't think you\nneed to start using it again.\n\n¢ Say you didn’t realise there were risks to using the reliever inhaler frequently. You'll\nfollow the advice and buy a new preventer inhaler.\n\ne Say you'll come back in four weeks.\n\n© Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessment (2022)\n\nOET SAMPLE TEST\nCANDIDATE CARD NO. 1 MEDICINE\n\nSETTING Doctor's Clinic\n\nDOCTOR This 32-year-old patient was diagnosed with adult-onset asthma one year ago. Dust mites\nwere identified as a major trigger. You are seeing the patient for his/her annual asthma review.\n\nTASK ¢ Find out about patient’s asthma symptoms (e.g., any change, effect on daily activities!\nsleep, etc.).\n\ne Review patient’s asthma triggers (e.g., exposure to dust mites, any control measures\ntried, etc.).\n\n¢ Explore patient's asthma self-management strategies (e.g., daily use of inhaled\ncorticosteroid preventer, frequency of salbutamol reliever use, etc.).\n\n¢ Explain risks of overusing salbutamol reliever (e.g., increased airway hyper-responsiveness,\nuncontrolled asthma, asthma-related hospital admission more likely, etc.). Advise patient to\npurchase replacement preventer.\n\ne Emphasise importance of using preventer daily (e.g., reduced swelling/inflammation in\nairways, lowered sensitivity to triggers, decreased mucus production, etc.).\n\ne Recommend follow-up appointment in four weeks (e.g., monitor treatment effect,\nreview medication, etc.).", "extraction": "ocr", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 2036.0, "height": 2800.0, "blocks": [{"text": "| Test3 | Speaking (approximately 20 minutes)", "lines": [{"text": "| Test3 | Speaking (approximately 20 minutes)", "x": 0.02259, "y": 0.01179, "width": 0.58055, "height": 0.04357, "confidence": 0.913, "words": [{"text": "|", "x": 0.02259, "width": 0.02014, "confidence": 0.862}, {"text": "Test3", "x": 0.06238, "width": 0.10511, "confidence": 0.862}, {"text": "|", "x": 0.19204, "width": 0.02554, "confidence": 0.81}, {"text": "Speaking", "x": 0.24263, "width": 0.13212, "confidence": 0.962}, {"text": "(approximately", "x": 0.3836, "width": 0.1169, "confidence": 0.962}, {"text": "20", "x": 0.50589, "width": 0.02161, "confidence": 0.969}, {"text": "minutes)", "x": 0.5334, "width": 0.06974, "confidence": 0.965}], "uncertainWords": []}]}, {"text": "OET SAMPLE TEST\nROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 1", "lines": [{"text": "OET SAMPLE TEST", "x": 0.3998, "y": 0.07964, "width": 0.24804, "height": 0.01714, "confidence": 0.952,